{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_vrtx_0153tjfQuftiBSnYcD4QsWEt","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# POV (Point of View) Voice Profile\n2\t# Strong opinion, advocacy, taking a stand\n3\t# Best for: Op-eds, blog posts with thesis, ADRs, position papers\n4\t\n5\tname: \"pov\"\n6\tversion: \"1.0\"\n7\tdescription: \"Strong opinion and advocacy voice for taking a clear stand\"\n8\t\n9\tcharacteristics:\n10\t  # Formality level: 0.0 = casual, 1.0 = formal\n11\t  # 0.5 = accessible but serious\n12\t  formality: 0.5\n13\t\n14\t  # Personality level: 0.0 = neutral/objective, 1.0 = opinionated/engaged\n15\t  # 0.9 = very high, strong opinions and convictions\n16\t  personality: 0.9\n17\t\n18\t  # Use first-person pronouns (I, we)\n19\t  first_person: true\n20\t\n21\t  # Use contractions (don't, can't, won't)\n22\t  contractions: true\n23\t\n24\t  # Target audience level\n25\t  audience: \"intermediate\"\n26\t\n27\tsentence_patterns:\n28\t  # Mix in short punchy sentences for emphasis\n29\t  mix_short: true\n30\t\n31\t  # Maximum consecutive sentences of similar length\n32\t  max_consecutive_similar: 2\n33\t\n34\t  # Target average sentence length\n35\t  avg_length_target: 16\n36\t\n37\t  # Sentence length variation controls natural rhythm\n38\t  # Use EITHER 'variation' level OR explicit 'min_length'/'max_length' range\n39\t  #\n40\t  # Variation levels (default: moderate):\n41\t  #   low      = ±4 words  (uniform, consistent)\n42\t  #   moderate = ±8 words  (natural variety)\n43\t  #   high     = ±12 words (dramatic rhythm shifts)\n44\t  #\n45\t  # Alternative: explicit range (overrides variation if set)\n46\t  #   min_length: 10\n47\t  #   max_length: 26\n48\t  variation: \"high\"\n49\t\n50\t  # Include rhetorical questions\n51\t  rhetorical_questions: true\n52\t\n53\telaboration:\n54\t  # How much to expand on concepts: minimal, moderate, thorough\n55\t  depth: \"moderate\"\n56\t\n57\t  # Include context and background before main points\n58\t  provide_context: true\n59\t\n60\t  # Add examples when helpful\n61\t  include_examples: true\n62\t\n63\t  # Explain the \"why\" not just the \"what\"\n64\t  explain_reasoning: true\n65\t\n66\tpersonality_traits:\n67\t  # Express opinions and reactions\n68\t  opinions: true\n69\t\n70\t  # Acknowledge uncertainty and complexity\n71\t  acknowledge_complexity: true\n72\t\n73\t  # Use humor sparingly\n74\t  humor: \"subtle\"\n75\t\n76\t  # Reference personal experience\n77\t  personal_experience: true\n78\t\n79\tpronoun_balance:\n80\t  # Target ratio of \"you\" vs \"we\" usage\n81\t  you_percentage: 50\n82\t  we_percentage: 50\n83\t\n84\tsignature_phrases:\n85\t  - \"Here's what I think\"\n86\t  - \"Let me be direct\"\n87\t  - \"This matters because\"\n88\t  - \"I'll argue that\"\n89\t  - \"The real issue is\"\n90\t  - \"Make no mistake\"\n91\t\n92\tavoid_phrases:\n93\t  - \"It goes without saying\"\n94\t  - \"As everyone knows\"\n95\t  - \"Obviously\"\n96\t  - \"Some might say\"\n97\t  - \"It could be argued\"\n98\t\n99\t# Example of this voice:\n100\t# \"Let me be direct: Kubernetes complexity is a feature, not a bug. I've\n101\t# heard the complaints about YAML sprawl and steep learning curves. Here's\n102\t# what I think. The complexity exists because distributed systems are hard.\n103\t# Full stop. Anyone promising you simple container orchestration is selling\n104\t# you a lie. The real issue isn't that Kubernetes is too complex. It's that\n105\t# we've been pretending distributed computing could ever be simple.\"\n106\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"fa74885b-025e-4bfa-ade6-74069ce3b63b","uuid":"3092d1a0-bac2-42b6-877b-66b0c891f0e7","timestamp":"2026-05-04T07:51:33.139Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/rhuss/Development/context-engineering/cc-prose/prose/knowledge-base/voice-templates/pov.yaml","content":"# POV (Point of View) Voice Profile\n# Strong opinion, advocacy, taking a stand\n# Best for: Op-eds, blog posts with thesis, ADRs, position papers\n\nname: \"pov\"\nversion: \"1.0\"\ndescription: \"Strong opinion and advocacy voice for taking a clear stand\"\n\ncharacteristics:\n  # Formality level: 0.0 = casual, 1.0 = formal\n  # 0.5 = accessible but serious\n  formality: 0.5\n\n  # Personality level: 0.0 = neutral/objective, 1.0 = opinionated/engaged\n  # 0.9 = very high, strong opinions and convictions\n  personality: 0.9\n\n  # Use first-person pronouns (I, we)\n  first_person: true\n\n  # Use contractions (don't, can't, won't)\n  contractions: true\n\n  # Target audience level\n  audience: \"intermediate\"\n\nsentence_patterns:\n  # Mix in short punchy sentences for emphasis\n  mix_short: true\n\n  # Maximum consecutive sentences of similar length\n  max_consecutive_similar: 2\n\n  # Target average sentence length\n  avg_length_target: 16\n\n  # Sentence length variation controls natural rhythm\n  # Use EITHER 'variation' level OR explicit 'min_length'/'max_length' range\n  #\n  # Variation levels (default: moderate):\n  #   low      = ±4 words  (uniform, consistent)\n  #   moderate = ±8 words  (natural variety)\n  #   high     = ±12 words (dramatic rhythm shifts)\n  #\n  # Alternative: explicit range (overrides variation if set)\n  #   min_length: 10\n  #   max_length: 26\n  variation: \"high\"\n\n  # Include rhetorical questions\n  rhetorical_questions: true\n\nelaboration:\n  # How much to expand on concepts: minimal, moderate, thorough\n  depth: \"moderate\"\n\n  # Include context and background before main points\n  provide_context: true\n\n  # Add examples when helpful\n  include_examples: true\n\n  # Explain the \"why\" not just the \"what\"\n  explain_reasoning: true\n\npersonality_traits:\n  # Express opinions and reactions\n  opinions: true\n\n  # Acknowledge uncertainty and complexity\n  acknowledge_complexity: true\n\n  # Use humor sparingly\n  humor: \"subtle\"\n\n  # Reference personal experience\n  personal_experience: true\n\npronoun_balance:\n  # Target ratio of \"you\" vs \"we\" usage\n  you_percentage: 50\n  we_percentage: 50\n\nsignature_phrases:\n  - \"Here's what I think\"\n  - \"Let me be direct\"\n  - \"This matters because\"\n  - \"I'll argue that\"\n  - \"The real issue is\"\n  - \"Make no mistake\"\n\navoid_phrases:\n  - \"It goes without saying\"\n  - \"As everyone knows\"\n  - \"Obviously\"\n  - \"Some might say\"\n  - \"It could be argued\"\n\n# Example of this voice:\n# \"Let me be direct: Kubernetes complexity is a feature, not a bug. I've\n# heard the complaints about YAML sprawl and steep learning curves. Here's\n# what I think. The complexity exists because distributed systems are hard.\n# Full stop. Anyone promising you simple container orchestration is selling\n# you a lie. The real issue isn't that Kubernetes is too complex. It's that\n# we've been pretending distributed computing could ever be simple.\"\n","numLines":106,"startLine":1,"totalLines":106}}}
{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-4-6", "id": "msg_vrtx_01QjQnky9cENu4DKhE7DPovB", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "thinking", "thinking": "Now I have the POV voice profile. Let me rewrite the source text applying:\n1. The humanizer patterns (removing all AI slop)\n2. The POV voice (strong opinions, first person, contractions, rhetorical questions, high variation in sentence length)\n\nThe source text is absolutely riddled with AI patterns:\n- \"garnered significant attention\" (AI vocabulary)\n- \"Experts argue\" / \"some critics argue\" (weasel words)\n- \"crucial role in enhancing\" (AI vocabulary + superficial -ing)\n- \"It is important to note\" (filler)\n- \"showcases\" (AI vocabulary)\n\n\n- \"landscape\" (AI vocabulary)\n- \"interplay\" / \"intricate tapestry\" (AI vocabulary)\n- \"Additionally\" (AI vocabulary)\n- \"leverage\" (corporate speak)\n- \"well-positioned to navigate the evolving landscape\" (AI vocabulary)\n- \"In order to achieve\" (filler)\n- \"foster\" (AI vocabulary)\n- \"ensuring that\" (superficial -ing)\n- \"serves as a complement\" (copula avoidance)\n- \"The future looks bright\" (generic positive conclusion)\n- \"Exciting times lie ahead\" (generic positive conclusion)\n- \"journey toward\" (promotional)\n\nNow I'm ready to actually rewrite the source text with these patterns in mind.", "signature": ""}], "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 1, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 1301, "cache_read_input_tokens": 40471, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 1301, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 0}, "output_tokens": 8}, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "fa74885b-025e-4bfa-ade6-74069ce3b63b", "uuid": "bd9f15a7-e6df-473a-a72c-e3162b6ccfb2", "timestamp": "2026-05-04T07:51:42.112Z"}
{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-4-6", "id": "msg_vrtx_01QjQnky9cENu4DKhE7DPovB", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "The source text is wall-to-wall AI slop. Every sentence triggers multiple humanizer categories. Let me rewrite it with the POV voice (strong opinion, first person, punchy)."}], "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 1, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 1301, "cache_read_input_tokens": 40471, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 1301, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 0}, "output_tokens": 8}, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "fa74885b-025e-4bfa-ade6-74069ce3b63b", "uuid": "c0d84521-2f64-4438-912f-af97f2870d53", "timestamp": "2026-05-04T07:51:42.807Z"}
{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-4-6", "id": "msg_vrtx_01QjQnky9cENu4DKhE7DPovB", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "tool_use", "id": "toolu_vrtx_01HEw1TVqJhb1dQRBksNYNN7", "name": "Edit", "input": {"replace_all": false, "file_path": "/private/var/folders/3v/16m6pn090hl4d60grm1057ww0000gn/T/agent-eval/2026-05-04-opus/cases/case-003-opinion-piece-pov-voice/source.md", "old_string": "The recent trend toward AI-generated code has garnered significant attention from industry observers and practitioners alike. Experts argue that these tools play a crucial role in enhancing developer productivity, while some critics argue that the quality of generated code could potentially be problematic.\n\nIt is important to note that the technology showcases both promise and peril. The landscape of AI-assisted development is evolving rapidly, and it could be argued that the implications are far-reaching. Additionally, the interplay between human creativity and machine efficiency highlights the intricate tapestry of modern software engineering.\n\nOrganizations that leverage these tools effectively will be well-positioned to navigate the evolving landscape. In order to achieve optimal outcomes, teams must foster a culture of responsible AI adoption, ensuring that the technology serves as a complement to human expertise rather than a replacement.\n\nThe future looks bright for AI-assisted development. Exciting times lie ahead as the industry continues its journey toward a new paradigm of collaborative intelligence.", "new_string": "Here's what I think about AI-generated code: it's a power tool, not a magic wand. I've used Copilot and Claude Code daily for over a year now. Some days they save me an hour. Other days I spend that hour cleaning up the mess they made.\n\nThe productivity numbers don't tell the full story. Yes, I write boilerplate faster. But have you ever blindly accepted a suggestion, watched it pass CI, then discovered three weeks later it was subtly wrong? I have. More than once.\n\nThe real issue isn't whether AI coding tools are good or bad. That's a boring question. What matters is whether your team knows when to trust the output and when to throw it away. Most don't. They accept suggestions like autocomplete, without reading them, because the code looks plausible and the tests pass.\n\nI'll argue that the teams getting value from these tools are the ones that already had strong engineering practices. Good test coverage. Meaningful code review. Clear architectural boundaries. AI assistants amplify whatever you already have. If your codebase is a mess, they'll generate more mess, faster.\n\nSo stop asking whether to adopt AI coding tools. Start asking whether your team can tell good code from bad code without them. If the answer is no, that's your actual problem."}}], "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 1, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 1301, "cache_read_input_tokens": 40471, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 1301, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 0}, "output_tokens": 8}, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "fa74885b-025e-4bfa-ade6-74069ce3b63b", "uuid": "bd3b7334-f694-45a6-b64b-3369896986a5", "timestamp": "2026-05-04T07:51:57.708Z"}
